From e9bceb3936c80b6d0f362454c15b7c62e34c71b4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vortiago <atle@havso.net>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 15:47:52 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] perf(ggml): align large allocations to 2 MiB and hint
 MADV_HUGEPAGE on Linux
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

On Linux, ggml_aligned_malloc now aligns an allocation of 4 MiB or more
to 2 MiB and hints it with MADV_HUGEPAGE, so the kernel can back it with
transparent huge pages. This covers the CPU KV cache, the recurrent
state and the compute buffers. Mmapped weights do not pass through this
function. If the 2 MiB alignment fails, the allocation falls back to the
ordinary alignment.

glibc serves a request below its dynamic mmap threshold from the heap,
and that threshold rises to 32 MiB after a large free. A 4 to 32 MiB
buffer can therefore sit on the heap, and the hint then marks heap pages
that malloc reuses later. That can raise RSS through khugepaged. With
defrag=madvise, the first touch of a large KV cache can also stall in
direct compaction. Measure startup time and compact_stall in
/proc/vmstat before keeping this commit.

Changes from upstream: the madvise call sits under #ifdef
MADV_HUGEPAGE, because some libc headers do not define it.
<sys/mman.h> moves to the other includes. The comment above
GGML_LARGE_ALLOC_* states the heap caveat in place of the note on an
unexplained mechanism.

ggml.c is in ggml-base, so this affects the host buffers of every
backend. It is a separate commit so it can be measured and dropped on
its own.

Ported from upstream llama.cpp PR #27478 (the ggml.c part).

